-- title:
--   Inverse Frobenius re-basing raises the transport exponent by one
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ and $\bar r$ are primes with $\bar r \neq r$, and $N$ is a nonzero natural number with $r \nmid N$.
--
--   Coefficients. $\mathcal{O}$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), $\pi \in \mathcal{O}$ is irreducible, $\mathcal{O}$ is adically complete for the ideal $(\pi)$, the residue ring $\mathcal{O}/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements, and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal{O}$; $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero field which is a fraction field of $\mathcal{O}$. Further, $Onr$ is a characteristic-zero domain and an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra, $Fr$ is an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra automorphism of $Onr$, and the following are assumed: $Onr$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal{O}$ modulo it (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo it (`hOnr_closed`), and $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r}$ modulo it for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   Quaternionic data. For rationals $a, b$, the hypothesis `hB` asserts that $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ satisfies `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b r rbar`, i.e. $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ the completed algebra is a division algebra precisely when $v$ lies above $r$ or above $\bar r$. $\Lambda$ is a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule which is a maximal order (`hΛ`) and contains every rational integer (`hΛℤ`), and $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to \mathbb{Z}_{r^2} \times \mathbb{Z}_{r^2}$ (values in Witt vectors over $\mathbb{F}_{r^2}$) satisfies `IsOrderCoord`: additivity, $1 \mapsto (1,0)$, the Frobenius-twisted multiplication rule, injectivity, density of the image modulo every power of $r$, and the trace condition.
--
--   The base point in characteristic $r$. $A_0$ is a fake elliptic curve with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure over $Onr/(\pi)$, $X_0$ is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $Onr/(\pi)$ of height $4$, $\theta_0$ are formal coordinates of dimension $2$ for $A_0 \to \operatorname{Spec}(Onr/(\pi))$, and `hA₀` states that $\theta_0$ are formal coordinates for the group law of $A_0$ with formal group $X_0.F$ and that the $\Lambda$-action is computed by $\mathrm{coord}$ through $X_0$.
--
--   The formal-module frame. $\iota : \mathbb{Z}_{r^2} \to Onr$ is a ring homomorphism, $\Phi$ is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $Onr/(r)$ which is special for the reduction of $\iota$ (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`), $M$ is a moduli package over $(r, Onr)$ satisfying the Zariski-sheaf condition `hM`, and $\eta$ assigns to every ring $B'$ with a homomorphism $\psi' : Onr \to B'$ and $r$ nilpotent in $B'$ a map from $\Phi$-rigidified objects over $B'$ to $M.\mathrm{obj}\,B'\,\psi'$. The hypothesis `hη` has three clauses: over Noetherian bases, $\eta$ identifies two admissible rigidified objects exactly when they are isomorphic; $\eta$ is natural in the base; and every point of $M$ is, on a finite Zariski cover of the base by localisations, in the image of $\eta$ on admissible objects. $E_0$ is a ring homomorphism from the centraliser of the endomorphisms $\Phi.\mathrm{actEnd}$ together with $\Phi.\mathrm{varpiEnd}$ into $\mathrm{M}_2(K_0)$, and `hE₀` requires $E_0$ injective together with an exponent $m$ such that $r^{m}$ times any matrix over $\mathcal{O}$ lies in the image, and $r^{m}$ times any value of $E_0$ is a matrix over $\mathcal{O}$.
--
--   Reduction and transport data. $\kappa : Onr/(\pi) \to Onr/(r)$ is a ring homomorphism compatible with the two quotient maps (`hκ`), $n_0$ is a natural number and $\beta_0$ a pair of power series in two variables over $Onr/(r)$ which is an isogeny $\Phi \to X_0 \otimes_\kappa (Onr/(r))$ of height $4n_0$ (`hβ₀`).
--
--   The comparison map $\Xi$. $\Xi$ assigns to each Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B'$, each $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\psi' : Onr \to B'$ with $\pi$ nilpotent in $B'$, and each rigidified curve over $(B', \psi')$ a $G$-point of $M$ over $\mathcal{O}$. Four hypotheses on $\Xi$ are imposed, summarised here: `hΞleg`, over a base with only the trivial idempotents, the $Onr$-leg of $\Xi$ is $\mathrm{frobTwist}\,Onr\,Fr\,k\,\psi' = \psi' \circ Fr^{k}$ for some $k \in \mathbb{Z}$; `hΞnat`, $\Xi$ commutes with pullback of rigidified curves along $\mathcal{O}$-algebra maps; `hΞiso`, $\Xi$ takes equal values on two rigidified curves over the same $(B', \psi')$ whose underlying curves are isomorphic via $i$ compatibly with the group law, the $\Lambda$-action and the level structure, provided the rigidification data are matched by maps $ib$ on the $E_b$-legs and $uA$ on the $A_b$-legs with the stated compatibilities and with $ib$ followed by $\varphi'$, $uA$ and multiplication by $r^{i_1}$ agreeing with $\varphi$ followed by multiplication by $r^{j_1}$ for some exponents $i_1, j_1$; `hΞdef`, over a connected Noetherian base in which $\pi$ and $r$ are nilpotent, each rigidified curve equipped with formal coordinates admits $j \le 1$ and a rigidified object $t$ with $t.X$ the given formal module, with `IsRigTransport` $\theta_0\,\kappa\,\beta_0$ holding for the rigidification, the coordinates and the exponent $j$, with $t$ admissible for $\iota$ and the twist $\psi' \circ Fr^{-j}$, and with $\Xi$ of the curve equal to the $G$-point with leg $\psi' \circ Fr^{-j}$ and point $\eta(t)$.
--
--   The inverse Frobenius twist of the base point. $A_{0r}$ is a fake elliptic curve over $Onr/(\pi)$ together with $\mathrm{prA} : A_{0r}.A \to A_0.A$ exhibiting $A_{0r}$ as the pullback of $A_0$ along the residue map induced by $Fr^{-1}$ (`hprA`). Maps $F : A_0.A \to A_{0r}.A$ and $V : A_{0r}.A \to A_0.A$ over the base are given, each compatible with the relative group laws (`F_hom`, `V_hom`), equivariant for the $\Lambda$-actions (`F_act`, `V_act`) and preserving the level structures (`F_lev`, `V_lev`), with $F$ followed by $V$ equal to multiplication by $r$ on $A_0$ (`V_F`) and $V$ followed by $F$ equal to multiplication by $r$ on $A_{0r}$ (`F_V`); finally `V_frob` states that for every ring $C$ of characteristic $r$ and every point $x$ of $A_{0r}$ over $C$, $x$ followed by $V$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the $r$-power Frobenius of $C$ followed by $x$ followed by $\mathrm{prA}$, so that $V$ is the relative Frobenius.
--
--   The local situation. $B$ is a Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra with an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\psi : Onr \to B$, with the image of $\pi$ nilpotent in $B$ and with only the trivial idempotents (`hconn`). $E$ is a fake elliptic curve over $B$, $\rho$ is a rigidification of $E$ relative to $A_0$ over $(B,\psi)$, and $\rho^{-}$ (written `ρp`) is a rigidification of the same $E$ over $(B, \psi \circ Fr^{-1})$. The two are linked by: $ub : \rho.E_b.A \to \rho^{-}.E_b.A$ with $ub$ followed by $\rho^{-}.g_b$ equal to $\rho.g_b$ and $ub$ over the base; $gA' : \rho^{-}.A_b.A \to A_{0r}.A$ exhibiting $\rho^{-}.A_b$ as the pullback of $A_{0r}$ along the residue map of $\psi$, with $gA'$ followed by $\mathrm{prA}$ equal to $\rho^{-}.g_A$; $F_b : \rho.A_b.A \to \rho^{-}.A_b.A$ over the base with $F_b$ followed by $gA'$ equal to $\rho.g_A$ followed by $F$; $V_b : \rho^{-}.A_b.A \to \rho.A_b.A$ over the base with $V_b$ followed by $\rho.g_A$ equal to $gA'$ followed by $V$; the degree shift $\rho^{-}.d = \rho.d + 1$; $ub$ followed by $\rho^{-}.\varphi$ equal to $\rho.\varphi$ followed by $F_b$; and $\rho^{-}.\varphi' = V_b$ followed by $\rho.\varphi'$ followed by $ub$.
--
--   Coordinates and the given transport. $X$ is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $B$, $\theta$ are formal coordinates of dimension $2$ for $E \to \operatorname{Spec} B$, and `hX` states that $E$ is a formal module via $\mathrm{coord}$, $X$ and $\theta$. Finally $j'$ is a natural number and $t'$ a $\Phi$-rigidified object over $B$ whose series $t'.\rho$ has vanishing constant coefficients in each component (`ht'0`), and `htr'` asserts `IsRigTransport` $\theta_0\,\kappa\,\beta_0\,\rho^{-}\,\theta\,j'\,t'$.
--
--   Conclusion: `IsRigTransport` $\theta_0\,\kappa\,\beta_0\,\rho\,\theta\,(j'+1)\,t'$ holds; that is, there exist a ring homomorphism $\kappa_B : B/(\pi) \to B/(r)$ and a pair of power series $\sigma$ in two variables over $B/(\pi)$ such that: $\kappa_B$ precomposed with the quotient map $B \to B/(\pi)$ is the quotient map $B \to B/(r)$; $\kappa_B$ precomposed with the residue map $Onr/(\pi) \to B/(\pi)$ induced by $\psi$ equals the reduction of $\psi$ modulo $r$ precomposed with $\kappa$; for every ring $B''$ that is simultaneously an algebra over $B/(\pi)$, over $B$ and over $Onr/(\pi)$ in such a way that the structure map of $B$ factors through $B/(\pi)$ and the structure map of $Onr/(\pi)$ factors through the residue map of $\psi$, for every ideal $J$ of $B''$ and every $m$ with $J^{m+1} = 0$, every $s : \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to J$ and every point $P_A : \operatorname{Spec} B'' \to \rho.A_b.A$ over the structure map of $B''$ with $P_A$ followed by $\rho.g_A$ equal to the point $\theta_0(s)$, one has $P_A$ followed by $\rho.\varphi'$ and $\rho.g_b$ equal to the point $\theta$ evaluated at the truncated values $\mathrm{nilEval}\,m\,(\sigma_i)\,s$; and $t'.\rho$ equals $\sigma$ reduced along $\kappa_B$, composed with $\beta_0$ reduced along $\psi$, composed with the substitution $X_i \mapsto X_i^{\,r^{\,j'+1}}$.
--
--   This is the Frobenius re-basing step in the supersingular dictionary underlying the Čerednik–Drinfeld description of a Shimura curve at $r$: passing from the rigidification $\rho^{-}$ over the $Fr^{-1}$-twisted structure map to $\rho$ itself replaces the transport exponent $j'$ by $j'+1$, the extra power of $r$ coming from the relative Frobenius $V$ on the base point $A_0$. It is used in the comparison of the level indices $n$ of two transports and in the construction of the scalar $r$-power action on the $G$-point attached to a rigidified curve.
